  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe lake effect rain and snow showers that have been impacting \nnorthwest Indiana over the past few days have moved east and out\nof our CWA region. Northwest Indiana is still under lake effect\ncloud cover currently, which may persist until later tomorrow \nevening, when skies eventually will clear out. For the rest of \nthe CWA, clear skies and seasonable temperatures will be what we\nsee through the weekend and the beginning of next week.\n\nTemperatures tonight will fall into the mid to upper 20s \nacross most of Illinois and western northwest Indiana. If the \nlake effect cloud cover does stay in parts of northwest Indiana,\ntemperatures will stay warmer, likely staying just above \nfreezing. After the lake effect clouds depart the area fully, \nlikely by Sunday evening, conditions look to turn sunny and dry \nthrough at least next Wednesday. \n\nOver the next few days, temperatures will begin to warm to near\nnormal tomorrow, with high temperatures rising up to the upper\n40s and low 50s tomorrow. Next week, temperatures will steadily\nrise into the upper 50s and low 60s, well above normal for mid\nNovember. Conditions look to remain dry and sunny through\nWednesday as well.\n\nParsons/ENI",
